  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n- Brief chance of rain Friday night; then warm and sunny Saturday\n\nReally no changes with the forecast through Saturday night for the \narea. various sources of model data all show a small and brief \nchance of rain coming through Friday evening into early Friday \nnight. This remains associated with a sfc warm front and low level \njet core of almost 30 knots passing through. Rainfall with this will \nbe quite limited as moisture below 6k ft is quite limited, as is the \nwindow of opportunity with the forcing.\n\nIt will be noticeable on Saturday that we will be in the warm sector \nwith steady warm winds blowing, and sfc temperatures well into the \n70s. 850 mb temperatures around 16C will support those warm October \ntemperatures. \n\n- Rain chances with Isaias diminishing for Sunday\n\nThe most uncertain time frame in the forecast for the last few days \nhas been centered around the Sunday time frame, due to the \nuncertainty with \"Isaias\" and the upper low that will be merging \nwith it as it becomes extra-tropical in nature after making landfall \nalong the Gulf coast.\n\nThe latest trend for it is to turn eastward a little quicker, and \nbringing just a chance of rain to the SE portion of the forecast \narea. We have been saying for days that the merging with a non-\ntropical upper low, and the movement of upper lows is quite \nuncertain. The 00z runs of the global models all trended east with \nit, after having trended NNW the previous couple of days before \nthat. The latest trend is likely due to the atmosphere to its west \nbeing blocked up with a stronger ridge over the Mississippi River \nValley, and the strong upper trough over the Western U.S..\n\n- Strong cold front Monday night/Tuesday with colder air to follow\n\nThe other trend we have noticed is that the rainfall amounts with \nthe stronger cold front coming through Monday night and Tuesday have \ndiminished a bit. The timing of the cold front has remained similar \nas previous runs, but qpf is on the lighter side overall. This looks \nto be a result from a lack of good Gulf moisture with Isaias taking \nmuch of it, and another good moisture feed not able to become \nestablished with the ridge immediately out ahead of it. \n\nMuch of the precipitation also looks to be right along, and behind \nthe front as short waves in the upper flow ride along the front. We \nwill see much cooler air advect in on the low level flow from the NE \naround an area of Canadian high pressure. \n\nBeyond Tuesday/Wednesday has become more complicated as the large \nupper trough could possibly close off a low over the SW U.S., and \npotentially send more energy to the NE.",
